// Practice quiz: Configuration files and profiles
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/porting-and-configuration/configuration-files-and-profiles

::o1 typed binding::Which annotation binds several related settings into a typed record? {
=@ConfigurationProperties#Correct. It binds a whole prefix.
~@Value#Not quite. @Value reads a single value.
~@Bean#Not quite. @Bean registers an object.
~@Profile#Not quite. @Profile limits when a bean exists.
}

::o1 name matching::A record bound with prefix app.mail has a component host. Which key sets it? {
=app.mail.host#Correct. The prefix plus the component name.
~host#Not quite. The prefix is required.
~mail.app.host#Not quite. The prefix order matters.
~app.host.mail#Not quite. The key follows prefix then name.
}

::o2 profile override::With the dev profile active, which file overrides application.properties? {
=application-dev.properties#Correct. A profile-specific file overrides the base.
~dev.properties#Not quite. That name is not picked up automatically.
~application.dev#Not quite. That is not a recognized name.
~pom.xml#Not quite. That is a build file.
}

::o2 activation::Which property activates a profile? {
=spring.profiles.active#Correct. It names the active profile or profiles.
~spring.config.import#Not quite. That imports additional config.
~server.port#Not quite. That sets a port.
~spring.application.name#Not quite. That names the app.
}

::o3 precedence::A property is set in application.properties and in an environment variable, with no command-line value. Which wins? {
=The environment variable#Correct. Environment variables outrank packaged files.
~application.properties#Not quite. The file is only a default here.
~Neither; startup fails#Not quite. Overlap is normal.
~Whichever was edited last#Not quite. Timestamps do not matter.
}

